### What is the No-Repeat Substring problem?

The problem asks for the length of the longest contiguous substring within a given string where every character is distinct. For example, in `"ABCABCBB"`, the longest substring without repeating characters is `"ABC"` with length 3. In `"PWWKEW"`, it is `"WKE"` with length 3.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// Input: "TRANSFERPAY"
// Substrings with all unique chars: "TRANSFE", "RANSFEP", "ANSFERP", ...
// Longest: "TRANSFE" or "ANSFERPAY" depends on actual duplicates
```

### Why use a sliding window?

The brute-force approach checks every substring and tests for uniqueness, giving O(n^3) time with a set check or O(n^2) with precomputation. The sliding window reduces this to O(n) by maintaining a window that is always duplicate-free. The key insight is that when a duplicate enters, we do not need to restart from scratch: we just move the left boundary past the previous occurrence of the duplicated character.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// Brute force: O(n^3) - check every substring for uniqueness
// Optimized brute force: O(n^2) with set per window
// Sliding window: O(n) - each character visited at most twice
```

### How does the sliding window work?

You maintain a left boundary and a right pointer. A map stores the index of each character's most recent appearance. As the right pointer moves to each character, you check the map. If the character was last seen at a position at or after the left boundary, it is a duplicate inside the window, so you move the left boundary to one past that position. Then you update the character's position in the map and compute the current window length.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class NoRepeatSubstring {

    public int lengthOfLongestSubstring(String s) {
        Map<Character, Integer> lastSeen = new HashMap<>();
        int left = 0;
        int best = 0;

        for (int right = 0; right < s.length(); right++) {
            char c = s.charAt(right);
            int last = lastSeen.getOrDefault(c, -1);

            if (last >= left) {
                left = last + 1; // jump past the duplicate
            }

            lastSeen.put(c, right);
            best = Math.max(best, right - left + 1);
        }
        return best;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        NoRepeatSubstring solver = new NoRepeatSubstring();
        System.out.println(solver.lengthOfLongestSubstring("ABCABCBB"));
        System.out.println(solver.lengthOfLongestSubstring("BBBBB"));
        System.out.println(solver.lengthOfLongestSubstring("PWWKEW"));
    }
}
```

```text
3
1
3
```

### Why is the guard condition last >= left critical?

The map may contain stale entries for characters that were once in the window but are now to the left of the current left boundary. Without the guard `last >= left`, the left boundary could jump backward to an old position, which would make the window contain duplicates from a previous iteration. The guard ensures we only react to duplicates that are actually inside the current window.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// Example: s = "ABBA"
// right=0, c='A', last=-1, left stays 0, window "A"
// right=1, c='B', last=-1, left stays 0, window "AB"
// right=2, c='B', last=1, 1>=0, left=2, window "B"
// right=3, c='A', last=0, 0 < 2 (stale!), left stays 2, window "BA"

// Without guard: left would jump to 0+1=1, making window "BBA" with duplicate B!
```

### How do you use an int array instead of a HashMap?

For ASCII input, an `int[128]` array indexed by character value is faster than a HashMap because it avoids boxing, hashing, and collision resolution. Initialize all entries to -1 to indicate no character has been seen.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class NoRepeatSubstringFast {

    public int lengthOfLongestSubstring(String s) {
        int[] lastSeen = new int[128];
        java.util.Arrays.fill(lastSeen, -1);
        int left = 0;
        int best = 0;

        for (int right = 0; right < s.length(); right++) {
            char c = s.charAt(right);
            if (lastSeen[c] >= left) {
                left = lastSeen[c] + 1;
            }
            lastSeen[c] = right;
            best = Math.max(best, right - left + 1);
        }
        return best;
    }
}
```

### What are the edge cases?

Edge cases include: an empty string returns 0, a string of all identical characters returns 1, a string with all unique characters returns the full length, and a single character returns 1.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// Edge case 1: "" -> 0 (empty string)
// Edge case 2: "AAAA" -> 1 (all same characters)
// Edge case 3: "ABCDEF" -> 6 (all unique)
// Edge case 4: "A" -> 1 (single character)
// Edge case 5: "AU" -> 2 (two unique characters)
```

### What about a banking use case?

At the National Bank of India, each customer session records an ordered sequence of transaction-channel codes. The fraud-analytics team measures the longest continuous stretch where no channel repeats, because a long diverse-channel run is a stable behavioral fingerprint. A sudden drop signals possible account takeover.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class FraudChannelAnalyzer {

    private final NoRepeatSubstring solver = new NoRepeatSubstring();

    public int longestDiverseStretch(String channelSequence) {
        return solver.lengthOfLongestSubstring(channelSequence);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        FraudChannelAnalyzer analyzer = new FraudChannelAnalyzer();

        String normalSession = "AUNWMCPT";  // ATM, UPI, NEFT, Web, Mobile, Cheque, POS, Transfer
        String suspiciousSession = "AAUUMM";

        System.out.println("Normal diverse stretch: "
            + analyzer.longestDiverseStretch(normalSession));
        System.out.println("Suspicious diverse stretch: "
            + analyzer.longestDiverseStretch(suspiciousSession));
    }
}
```

```text
Normal diverse stretch: 8
Suspicious diverse stretch: 2
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is forgetting the `last >= left` guard, which causes the left boundary to jump backward on stale map entries. Another mistake is using a set instead of a map, which requires an inner loop to remove characters one by one from the left, turning the solution into O(2n) instead of O(n) with direct jumps.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// WRONG: using a set with inner removal loop
Set<Character> set = new HashSet<>();
while (set.contains(s.charAt(right))) {
    set.remove(s.charAt(left));
    left++;  // O(n) inner loop in worst case
}
// Still correct but slower: O(2n) vs O(n) with direct jump

// CORRECT: direct jump using map
int last = lastSeen.getOrDefault(c, -1);
if (last >= left) left = last + 1;  // O(1) jump
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer should recognize this as the canonical variable-size sliding window problem. The same template applies to many string problems: longest substring with at most k distinct characters, longest substring with at most k replacements, and minimum window substring. The key difference between them is only the window validity condition.

For production use on very long strings, the int[128] array version avoids HashMap overhead and is measurably faster. The space is always O(alphabet size), not O(n).

